---
import Layout from '../layouts/Layout.astro';
---
<Layout
  title="for educators — tiny soft computer"
  description="Full setup and troubleshooting reference for educators preparing tiny soft computer kits ahead of a workshop."
  active="educators"
>

  <section class="hero" style="padding-top: 2.5rem;">
    <p class="kicker">for educators</p>
    <h1>this is prep work, not the workshop</h1>
    <p class="lede">
      this page is for whoever is setting up kits ahead of time, installing
      CircuitPython, flashing code, and testing hardware. attendees never
      see any of this. by the time someone sits down at the workshop, their
      kit is already fully preloaded and working. all they do is tweak the
      code, wire the button, and build their enclosure, see
      <a href="/workshop">workshop day</a> for that shorter version.
    </p>
    <p class="lede">
      what you'll need: an Adafruit RP2040 Feather ThinkInk, a 2.13" 212×104
      flexible monochrome eInk display (ILI0373 chipset), a USB-C cable,
      and a computer to program from. for a full parts list and
      alternatives, see <a href="/resources">resources</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>quick reference: flashing a batch</h2>
    <p>
      if you've already been through the full setup once and just need a
      fast checklist for preparing multiple units, here it is. full
      detail on each step is below.
    </p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>disconnect the eInk display before entering bootloader mode, for every board.</li>
      <li>flash CircuitPython 10.x, if it isn't already installed.</li>
      <li>copy <code>lib/adafruit_il0373.mpy</code> and <code>lib/adafruit_display_text/</code> onto the board.</li>
      <li>copy the final <a href="/code">code.py</a> onto the board.</li>
      <li>reconnect the display and test: the intro screen should appear, press the button once, and confirm it starts cycling through messages with the countdown printing on the serial console.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">
      keep a simple tally as you go, which units pass cleanly versus need
      troubleshooting. that tells you a lot about consistency across the
      batch before the workshop itself.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>1. install CircuitPython</h2>
    <p class="note">
      before you start: disconnect the eInk display from the Feather.
      having it connected can interfere with the board entering
      bootloader mode. reconnect it once CircuitPython is installed.
    </p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>unplug the board completely.</li>
      <li>press and hold the <strong>BOOTSEL</strong> button (this is the button on the board itself, labeled BOOT or BOOTSEL, not the reset button).</li>
      <li>while still holding it, plug in the USB-C cable.</li>
      <li>wait a second or two, then release the button. this "boot on start" method is more reliable than double-pressing reset while already powered on.</li>
      <li>a drive named <code>RPI-RP2</code> should appear. check your file browser (Finder on Mac, File Explorer on Windows) under "Locations" or "This PC", it shows up like a USB flash drive.</li>
      <li>go to <a href="https://circuitpython.org">circuitpython.org</a>, click the <strong>Downloads</strong> tab in the nav, and search for your board: "Adafruit Feather RP2040 ThinkInk". don't pick a generic RP2040 file, other variants have different pin mappings.</li>
      <li>choose the stable release (10.x), and download the single <code>.uf2</code> file. it will land in your computer's <strong>Downloads</strong> folder unless you chose a different save location.</li>
      <li>open your Downloads folder, find that <code>.uf2</code> file, and drag it onto the <code>RPI-RP2</code> drive.</li>
      <li>the board restarts automatically. the drive is now named <code>CIRCUITPY</code>, that means CircuitPython is installed.</li>
      <li>now reconnect the eInk display.</li>
    </ol>
    <p class="note">
      if the <code>RPI-RP2</code> drive never appears: confirm the display
      is disconnected, try a different USB-C cable (some are charge-only),
      try a different port, or unplug fully and retry the BOOTSEL sequence.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>2. add the required libraries</h2>
    <p>
      go to the <a href="https://circuitpython.org/libraries">CircuitPython
      Library Bundle</a> page, and download the zip matching your exact
      CircuitPython version (check <code>boot_out.txt</code> on your
      CIRCUITPY drive if you're not sure which version you're running).
      it will land in your <strong>Downloads</strong> folder. unzip it,
      then from inside the unzipped folder's <code>lib</code> subfolder,
      drag these two items onto your CIRCUITPY drive, into a folder there
      also named <code>lib</code> (create that folder if it doesn't exist
      yet):
    </p>
    <ul class="plain">
      <li><code>adafruit_il0373.mpy</code></li>
      <li><code>adafruit_display_text</code> (folder)</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="note">
      names must be exact, underscores not hyphens. a mismatched name is
      the most common reason a library fails to import.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>3. add the code</h2>
    <p class="note">
      when you first open <code>code.py</code> on a fresh CIRCUITPY drive,
      you'll likely see a single line already there:
      <code>print("Hello World!")</code>. that's just a default test file,
      not part of this project. delete it, then paste in the real code
      below.
    </p>
    <ol class="steps">
      <li>if you don't already have a code editor for CircuitPython, install <a href="https://codewith.mu/en/download">Mu Editor</a>, a free, beginner-friendly editor built specifically for it. you'll use it to edit and paste in code, and to view the serial console (see <a href="#serial-monitor">using the serial monitor</a> below).</li>
      <li>copy <a href="/code">the code</a> onto your CIRCUITPY drive as <code>code.py</code>, replacing whatever was there. (use the copy button on the code page to grab the whole file at once.)</li>
      <li>the board should auto-run it. if not, press reset once.</li>
      <li>watch the serial console as it runs, it prints its progress at each step.</li>
      <li>wait for "refresh complete" before judging what's on screen. e-ink flickers through intermediate frames mid-refresh, this can look like smudging and is completely normal.</li>
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>hardware: where things connect</h2>
    <div class="diagram-placeholder">
      <p>pin diagram coming soon</p>
      <p class="note" style="margin: 0;">
        will highlight: <code>A2</code> (button signal), <code>GND</code>
        (button ground), and the 24-pin eInk display connector
      </p>
    </div>
    <p>
      the button needs two wires: one leg to pin <code>A2</code>, the other
      leg to any <code>GND</code> pin. no resistor is needed, the code
      already uses the board's internal pull-up resistor.
    </p>
    <div class="note">
      most small tactile buttons have 4 legs in 2 internally-connected
      pairs. your two wires need to land on opposite pairs, not two legs
      from the same side, otherwise the button reads as permanently
      pressed (or never pressed) no matter what. if this happens, try
      moving one wire to a different leg and test again.
    </div>
    <p>
      the eInk display connects via its 24-pin ribbon cable into the
      Feather's display connector, this only fits one way, don't force it.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <h2 id="serial-monitor">using the serial monitor</h2>
    <p>
      the serial console (accessed through the REPL in Mu, or any serial
      terminal) shows live text output from the board while your code
      runs. this project's code prints a line at almost every step
      ("starting up", "display ready", "showing: ...", etc.), specifically
      so you can see exactly where something goes wrong instead of
      guessing.
    </p>
    <p>
      if a kit isn't behaving, check the serial console first, before
      touching any wiring. an error message here tells you far more than
      staring at the display ever will.
    </p>
  </section>

  <div class="divider">✿</div>

  <section class="block">
    <h2>troubleshooting notes</h2>
    <p>real issues we hit while testing, and what fixed them.</p>

    <div class="reference">
      <h3>why the display can't refresh quickly</h3>
      <p>
        this isn't a bug, it's physics. e-ink works by physically moving
        charged ink particles inside each pixel, not by lighting up
        transistors. that movement takes real time, and it can't be
        rushed by better code.
      </p>
      <p>
        the manufacturer's own datasheet for this exact panel states it
        directly: <em>"each update interval time should be minimum at
        180 seconds"</em> for the panel's rated 5-year, 1,000,000-cycle
        lifespan to hold. the CircuitPython driver enforces this,
        attempting a refresh before that window passes raises a
        <code>RuntimeError: Refresh too soon</code>, on purpose.
      </p>
      <p>
        it's possible to override this in code (a <code>refresh_time</code>
        parameter exists), but doing so doesn't make the physical ink
        move any faster, it just lets the code ask again sooner than the
        panel can actually deliver. the likely result is a ghosted or
        incomplete image, and real wear on the panel over repeated
        cycles. we chose not to override it.
      </p>
      <p>
        this is exactly the kind of limitation this workshop asks you to
        design with, not around. rather than fighting the display's pace,
        the wait itself becomes part of what pressing the button means,
        not a flaw to hide.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="reference">
      <h3>code runs with no error, but nothing shows</h3>
      <p>
        bitmaps must be created with <code>value_count=2</code>, not
        <code>1</code>. using <code>1</code> technically creates a
        zero-bit bitmap that can silently fail to render, even though
        the code runs without error. this was the root cause of a lot
        of confusing "it's not working" testing, not faulty hardware.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="reference">
      <h3>screen looks blurry or smudged</h3>
      <p>
        e-ink flickers through several frames during a refresh. don't
        judge the screen until the code confirms the refresh is
        complete. checking mid-refresh looks exactly like a real
        problem, and usually isn't one.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="reference">
      <h3>the RPI-RP2 drive won't appear</h3>
      <p>
        disconnect the eInk display before entering bootloader mode.
        the display connected during this step can prevent the drive
        from showing up at all.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="reference">
      <h3>SyntaxError referencing &lt;stdin&gt;</h3>
      <p>
        not a code problem. usually a stray keypress sent into the REPL
        prompt directly. press Ctrl+D to reload, or press the physical
        reset button, and try again.
      </p>
    </div>

    <div class="reference">
      <h3>a display seems consistently faulty</h3>
      <p>
        before assuming hardware failure: re-test with the latest known-good
        code first. an earlier bug in our own script caused some units to
        look broken when they weren't. if it's still inconsistent after
        that, isolate by swapping the display to a different board, and
        the board to a different display, whichever component the problem
        follows is the actual fault.
      </p>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section class="block">
    <p>
      this display holds its image with no power needed. once it shows
      text, you can even unplug it and the image will remain.
    </p>
    <p class="note">
      this is a living document. it gets updated as we test more units
      and find new issues, so it stays accurate for whoever builds one
      after us.
    </p>
    <p>
      for a full parts list and classroom-friendly alternatives, see
      <a href="/resources">resources</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

</Layout>